Glaciofluvial deposits
Soil properties for one of the hydrogeological environments described in Lundmark, A., 
2008. Monitoring transport and fate of de-icing salt in the roadside environment - Modelling 
and field measurements. Doctoral thesis in Land and Water Resources Engineering, 
TRITA-LWR PHD 1038. KTH Architecture and the Built Environment.

Conceptual description:
Glaciofluvial deposits occur mainly as eskers and consist of sand and gravel with varying 
thickness, but is typically 15-20 m. This coarse-grained soil is very permeable why the 
depth to groundwater can be large, up to 10-20 m. The vegetation is mainly coniferous 
forest, which is assumed to be dominated by pine, but farmland can also occur.

Soil profile:
Sand 0-2 m depth
Sand and gravel 2-20 m depth
Bedrock >20 m depth

Brooks and Corey parameters for sand represent effective parameters 
reported in Lundmark & Jansson: Generic soil descriptions for modelling water and chloride
dynamics in the unsaturated zone based on Swedish soils. Submitted December 2007 to
Geoderma. 

2008-03-17 Annika Lundmark 
